uages it's not  like the romance languages where this  basically with the romance languages i  have the feeling that it's one language  variations of one language  but but this  so far in my experience with with  russian check is that they're quite  different the structure is very similar  between russian and czech but the  vocabulary is tremendously different  yeah we have for example with there are  certain aspects of the grammar that  remain the same  um you know with with the perfective  imperfective verbs that's the theme  throughout the right the whole group  okay but even  but even even the cases for example in  bulgaria macedonia would have cases um  in czech and russian that's  a kind of a help yes um and the same  with polish  but then if you if you go to polish  you'd find it um interesting again  because  they have um  in the past tense where you've got the  atla form which have in russian and you  have in czech right  in polish they will they will add their  needs to that  for each individual person  they'll add which an ending so  and then they'll add these endings to  for the the you form and for the  the i form  okay well let me move on then to another  subject that was raised by one of the or  several people who follow my blog  because we're talking about vocabulary  in different languages  how do you learn vocabulary and how do  you keep up with this because we both  agree that  acquiring vocabulary is is the biggest  part like time wise  it's the biggest part  uh of learning a language i mean you  just need words i mean if if i don't  understand something it's not because i  don't understand the grammar mostly it's  because i don't have the word and if i  can't say something it's because i don't  have the word so the question is you've  got however many languages 16 i don't  remember the number at varying degrees  of proficiency  how do you learn the words how do you  retain them  yeah you're right the the words learning  vocabulary is probably on